dynarmic arm64: ISB on JIT entry, for code memory reused after a full clear

NOT BUILT, NOT RUN. Written and reviewed by reading only (2026-11-19).

Applies on top of dynarmic-pr2101-tree-shared-jit-cache-with-tests.patch (Lichii PR #2101 head
72ff982a3c plus that patch); checked with `git apply --check` on the file as that patch leaves it.
It adds to that patch and supersedes nothing.

Every Jit::Impl::Enter that is about to run code executes one ISB after the core is marked running.
A full clear runs only while no core is marked running, so every core passes this ISB between the
clear and running any code emitted over the reused memory. Entries happen once per Run/Step call,
not per block. Does not cover: a block published by another core while this core stays in the JIT
(the case the #2180 text argues is safe without an ISB, since that memory held no earlier code
since the last clear).

diff --git a/src/dynarmic/src/dynarmic/backend/arm64/a64_interface.cpp b/src/dynarmic/src/dynarmic/backend/arm64/a64_interface.cpp
index c4b3a9f..65d13ba 100644
--- a/src/dynarmic/src/dynarmic/backend/arm64/a64_interface.cpp
+++ b/src/dynarmic/src/dynarmic/backend/arm64/a64_interface.cpp
@@ -246,6 +246,12 @@ private:
     }
 };
 
+/// ISB: discards instructions this core fetched before code memory was rewritten by another core.
+/// (Inline assembly as in oaknut's CodeBlock::invalidate.)
+inline void InstructionSynchronizationBarrier() {
+    __asm__ volatile("isb" ::: "memory");
+}
+
 std::shared_ptr<SharedCodeCache> MakeSharedCodeCache() {
     return std::make_shared<SharedCodeCache>();
 }
@@ -464,6 +470,12 @@ private:
             }
             const CodePtr entry_point = GetBlock(descriptor);
             if (entry_point != cache->address_space->ReturnFromRunCode()) {
+                // Code memory is reused after a full clear, and other cores may have emitted and invalidated
+                // new code over it while this core was out of the JIT. Their invalidation is complete (their
+                // sys_icache_invalidate ends with DSB) and ordered before this point by the cache's locks and
+                // atomics, but this core may still hold instructions fetched from the old contents: a context
+                // synchronization event discards them. Entries are per Run or Step, not per block, so it is cheap.
+                InstructionSynchronizationBarrier();
                 return entry_point;
             }
             // The cache is full and other Jits run its code: wait for the clear.
